+/*  QUINTIC (S-CURVE) JERK-LIMITED RAMP
+    ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
+    Replaces the per-segment quantized jerk integration previously used here
+    (accumulate `last_accel` by jerk*dt each DT_SEGMENT tick, then guess when to
+    start ramping acceleration back down from a lookahead distance estimate).
+    That scheme had two structural problems, both removed by this one:
+
+      1. Acceleration was a staircase held constant for one segment and then
+         stepped, so the commanded jerk was a train of impulses at
+         ACCELERATION_TICKS_PER_SECOND rather than a bounded value. Shrinking
+         DT_SEGMENT reduced the step height but raised its frequency.
+
+      2. The ramp's terminal segment duration was recovered from the
+         trapezoidal identity time_var = 2*d/(v0 + v1). As exit_speed -> 0 that
+         denominator collapses and time_var blows up by orders of magnitude,
+         producing an erroneously long final segment (grblHAL discussion #384).
+
+    A quintic Bezier with control points P0=P1=P2=v0 and P3=P4=P5=v1 pins both
+    acceleration and jerk to zero at each end of the ramp and collapses exactly
+    to the smootherstep basis:
+
+        v(tau) = v0 + dv * P(tau),   P(tau) = 6*tau^5 - 15*tau^4 + 10*tau^3
+        s(tau) = S  * Snorm(tau),    Snorm   = (2*v0*tau + dv*Q(tau)) / (v0+v1)
+                                     Q(tau)  = tau^4 * (2*tau^2 - 6*tau + 5)
+
+    Because the integral of P over [0,1] is exactly 1/2, the ramp's mean
+    velocity is exactly (v0+v1)/2. The ramp therefore covers the planner's
+    trapezoidal distance budget S in exactly the trapezoidal duration
+
+        T = 2*S / (v0 + v1)
+
+    which is the same expression the linear path already uses at ramp
+    junctions. This is what lets the quintic drop into the existing
+    distance-budgeted profile without any geometric correction: the planner's
+    accelerate_until / decelerate_after / mm_complete boundaries are met
+    exactly, and the target speed is reached at exactly the same instant the
+    boundary is reached -- both are snapped, not approached.
+
+    The cost is in the derivatives. Peak acceleration is 15/8 = 1.875 times the
+    ramp's mean slope and peak jerk is (10/sqrt 3) * dv/T^2, so planner.c must
+    hand us an `acceleration` derated to keep both inside the axis limits --
+    see the a_eff derivation in plan_buffer_line(). Nothing here re-checks
+    those limits; honouring them is entirely a planner-side responsibility.
+
+    Terminal-segment stability falls out for free. time_var at ramp end is
+    (1 - tau)*T, drawn from the ramp's own duration which was fixed at ramp
+    start from the cruise speed. It never involves a division by a sum of
+    speeds that are both approaching zero, so the exit_speed -> 0 blowup and
+    the `last_time_var` continuity workaround are both gone.
+
+    Distance is evaluated ABSOLUTELY (mm_remaining = ramp_end + S*(1 - Snorm))
+    rather than by accumulating per-segment increments. Accumulating d1 - d0
+    differences of two large nearly-equal distances loses precision at small
+    DT_SEGMENT and lets rounding error walk the endpoint; the absolute form
+    cannot drift and lands on ramp_end bit-exactly.
+
+    NOTE: this shapes only the scalar "distance along block" clock. Per-axis
+    step distribution comes from st_prep_block->steps.value[idx] and
+    step_event_count, fixed once per block at load time and consumed by the
+    Bresenham DDA in the ISR. Multi-axis lockstep is therefore independent of
+    the velocity profile and unaffected by this change.
+*/
